/// How much of a PAID monthly AI allowance one company has already claimed.
///
/// ONE ROW PER COMPANY PER CALENDAR MONTH (UTC), and the row is a CLAIM
/// LEDGER, not a report. `AskUsage` next door records what a question cost
/// AFTER the model answered; this records that a unit was TAKEN before the
/// model was called at all. The two answer different questions and neither
/// can be derived from the other:
///
///   - AskUsage is written after the money is spent, and its write failure
///     is deliberately swallowed so a bookkeeping hiccup never costs
///     somebody their answer. That is correct for a courtesy limit and
///     useless for a paid cap: a process that dies mid-flight spends the
///     money and writes no row, so the next question is free.
///   - A row count is also the `max(n)+1` shape CLAUDE.md is built around.
///     Allowance derived from surviving rows is REISSUED the moment a row
///     is deleted. Nothing in this app deletes AskUsage today, and "today"
///     is exactly the word that made invoice numbering a bug for a year.
///   - Pages are not rows at all. A 40-page PDF is one AskUsage row and
///     forty units of a 300-page allowance.
///
/// So the numbers here only ever INCREMENT, inside the month they belong
/// to, and the claim is made with a conditional `updateMany`:
///
///     UPDATE ... SET questionsUsed = questionsUsed + n
///     WHERE questionsUsed <= cap - n
///
/// which is one statement, so two concurrent asks for the last unit cannot
/// both win — under Postgres READ COMMITTED the second re-evaluates the
/// WHERE against the first's committed row and matches zero rows. That is
/// the `InvoiceCounter` collision (#224) answered by design rather than
/// caught by a unique index.
///
/// NOT A SEQUENCE COUNTER, and deliberately not named `*Counter`: it issues
/// no numbers, nothing is unique on its value, and `counterCensus.test.ts`
/// is about counters that number a table. It is also COMPANY-scoped and
/// carries no `jobId`, so it is not a RESTRICT child of `Job` and the
/// scratch cleanup scripts — which delete rows under `Job` and `Contact`
/// and never touch `Company` — have nothing to do here. Same shape as
/// `AskUsage`, which is likewise absent from them.
///
/// ROLLOVER IS A NEW ROW, NEVER A RESET. Nothing ever decrements or zeroes
/// a period; next month is a different `periodStart` and therefore a
/// different row. A reset in place would be the retired-OSHA-case-number
/// mistake (issue #148) wearing a usage meter.
///
/// Append-and-increment only. Nothing deletes a period row.
model AskAllowancePeriod {
  id        String  @id @default(cuid())
  companyId String
  company   Company @relation(fields: [companyId], references: [id])

  /// Midnight UTC on the first of the month this row meters. The period is
  /// a UTC calendar month rather than a rolling 30 days because a person
  /// has to be able to answer "when does it come back" with a date, and
  /// "the 1st" is the only answer a rolling window cannot give.
  periodStart DateTime

  /// Questions CLAIMED this period, including ones whose model call then
  /// failed. Only increments.
  questionsUsed Int @default(0)
  /// Document pages CLAIMED this period, by the same rule.
  pagesUsed     Int @default(0)

  /// Of the above, the claims whose model call then failed — MARKED, never
  /// released. Releasing would make the cap defeatable by inducing
  /// failures, which is the whole reason the unit is claimed up front; and
  /// the provider bills the tokens of a stream that died halfway. These two
  /// figures are what makes a human credit possible: an owner can see on
  /// /settings/assistant that the month included failures and ask for them
  /// back. Nothing in this app adjusts an allowance automatically, and
  /// nothing bills for one.
  failedQuestions Int @default(0)
  failedPages     Int @default(0)

  /// PLAN SHEETS claimed this period, metered SEPARATELY from `pagesUsed`
  /// above and by the same claim-before-the-call rule.
  ///
  /// WHY A THIRD UNIT RATHER THAN MORE PAGES. Ingesting one 300-sheet plan set
  /// is 300 pages — the ENTIRE existing monthly page allowance — so a
  /// contractor who uploaded a drawing set would have no document allowance
  /// left for the compliance paperwork the same job needs, and the second set
  /// that month would be refused outright. The two are not the same product
  /// either: a page is a document somebody hands the assistant, and a sheet is
  /// a unit of a bulk job that runs unattended.
  ///
  /// Diego set the included figure at 1,500 sheets a month; the per-company
  /// value lives on `CompanyAiSettings.planSheetsPerMonth` so a plan can move
  /// it without a migration.
  ///
  /// `allowanceForCompany` DOES NOT READ IT, and this line said it did. That
  /// function returns `{questions, pages}` and ignores its `companyId` argument
  /// entirely — deliberately, since widening it would reshape a type the Ask box
  /// and the compliance upload share to carry a unit neither of them spends. The
  /// ceiling reaches the claim another way: `aiGate` already returns the company's
  /// `AiSettings`, which carries `planSheetsPerMonth`, so the gated caller hands it
  /// to `claimPlanSheet` (`lib/ask/planSheetSpend.ts`) and no page pays for a
  /// second query to learn it.
  planSheetsUsed   Int @default(0)
  /// Of those, sheets whose model call then failed — marked, never released,
  /// for exactly the reasons the two fields above are.
  failedPlanSheets Int @default(0)

  /// PAGES OF BID ADDENDA READ THIS MONTH — a fourth unit, and the argument is
  /// the third unit's argument arriving again rather than a new one.
  ///
  /// The obvious answer was to spend `pagesUsed`: an addendum is eight pages and
  /// the single-document ceiling is a hundred, so one fits comfortably. That
  /// checks the CEILING and never checks the MONTH. An estimator bidding twenty
  /// jobs with three addenda each at eight pages is ~480 pages against a 300-page
  /// month — one and a half times the whole allowance, spent on bids, leaving
  /// nothing for the lien waivers and certificates the won job then needs. Which
  /// is word for word why plan sheets are not pages, two fields up.
  ///
  /// The two are not the same product either. A document page is something
  /// somebody hands the assistant once; an addendum arrives on every bid, several
  /// times per bid, whether or not anybody wanted it that week.
  ///
  /// 600 a month is Diego's figure and A FIGURE IS NOT A MEASUREMENT — 480 is the
  /// modelled heavy month and 600 leaves room to re-read. Per-company on
  /// `CompanyAiSettings.addendumPagesPerMonth` so a plan can move it without a
  /// migration, and reaching the claim the way `planSheetsPerMonth` does: out of
  /// the `AiSettings` the gate already returned, never a second query.
  addendumPagesUsed   Int @default(0)
  /// Of those, pages whose model call then failed — marked, never released, for
  /// the reasons every other failure field here gives.
  failedAddendumPages Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// One row per company per month — and the constraint, not application
  /// code, is what makes that true when two questions arrive together.
  @@unique([companyId, periodStart])
}
